  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nMonday will once again be a similar story to Sunday in the southern \nportion of the state with highs in the mid 80s and winds gusting to \n20 mph. In the north, slight precipitation chances (10-20%) exist as \na shortwave moves across Manitoba/Ontario kicking off a weak \nsurface low pressure area and bringing an associated cold front\nthrough the area. The passage of the cold front will allow \nfall-like temperatures to return to the area with temperatures \ndropping back into the mid 60s to low 70s Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nBy the middle of next week, there is a signal for the return to\nan active period with multiple ensembles honing in on rain \nWednesday into Thursday. Models are in good agreement that a \nlarge trough will dig into the Rocky Mountains and then lift \ntowards the Midwest. There are significant differences between \nthe global models from run to run in the timing and phasing of \nthe trough. Those differences have large impacts on any weather \nthat will be experienced in Iowa. The European and Canadian \nmodels currently have a more open wave pattern that moves across\nthe area. While the GFS has a closed low that is weaker and \nslower to phase with the upper level flow. Model trends will be \nsomething to watch in the coming days to see how global models \nbegin to handle these variations in solutions.",
